---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";

const resolver = `
type FormResolver<TSchema, T, TPath extends string = string> =
  (schema: TSchema) => FormAdapter<T, TPath>;

interface FormAdapter<T, TPath extends string = string> {
  readonly fields: readonly FormFieldDescriptor[];
  validate(root: unknown): MaybeAsync<readonly FormIssue[]>;
}
`;

const descriptor = `
interface FormFieldDescriptor {
  readonly path: string;          // "owner.name", "items[*].quantity"
  readonly kind: FormFieldKind;   // string | number | boolean | date | array | object | unknown
  readonly isRequired: boolean;
  readonly constraints: FormFieldConstraints;    // minimum, maxLength, pattern, format, step...
  readonly choices?: readonly FormFieldChoice[]; // present only on a closed field
}
`;

const types = `
const adapter = zodFormResolver(
  z.object({ owner: z.object({ email: z.string() }) })
);

type Values = FormValues<typeof adapter>;   // { owner: { email: string } }
type Paths  = FormPaths<typeof adapter>;    // "owner" | "owner.email"

const good: Paths = "owner.email";
const typo: Paths = "owner.emial";          // compile error
`;

const asyncOne = `
const adapter = {
  fields: base.fields,
  async validate(root) {
    const local = base.validate(root);
    const taken = await isHandleTaken(root.handle);
    return taken
      ? [...local, { path: "handle", message: "すでに使われています" }]
      : local;
  },
};
`;

const packages = [
  ["form-contract", "The contract and the path types. No dependencies."],
  [
    "form-contract-resolver-zod",
    "Describes and judges a zod schema. zod is a type-only import, erased at build time.",
  ],
  ["form-core", "The runtime. No React, no validator."],
  ["form-react", "React bindings."],
  ["form-store-zustand", "A zustand-backed store, as a worked example of substituting one."],
];
---

<Base
  title="The contract — form-contract"
  description="A resolver is a plain function from a schema to a description of the form it implies. The value type survives it, so a misspelt path is a compile error."
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold sm:text-4xl">The contract</h1>

  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Two members. That is the whole thing.
  </p>

  <Code code={resolver} />

  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    A resolver is a plain function, named at the call site. There is no
    registry, no vendor tag and no dispatch, because whoever writes the call
    already knows which validator they are using. A validator able to describe
    its own fields implements this directly; one that cannot gets a resolver
    written for it once.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">One descriptor is one field</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Enough for a renderer to draw a field before any value exists.
  </p>

  <Code code={descriptor} />

  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Constraint values are carried <em>as declared</em>. A pattern stays a{" "}
    <code>RegExp</code>, because how a renderer spells one is that renderer's
    business.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">The type survives</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    <code>T</code> is the form's value type and <code>TPath</code> the paths that
    may be addressed, so a misspelt path is a compile error rather than a field
    that silently never renders.
  </p>

  <Code code={types} />

  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Carrying the type is what keeps the contract neutral about direction. A
    type-first validator passes the type its rules were written against; a
    schema-first one passes what its schema infers. Both arrive as the same
    form.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">A validator that has to ask something</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    <code>validate</code> may return a promise. A vendor that answers
    synchronously returns the list itself and nothing downstream pays for the
    possibility — which is why this is one member that <em>may</em> be async
    rather than a second member that always is.
  </p>

  <Code code={asyncOne} />

  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Passes are numbered, so an answer overtaken by a newer one is dropped rather
    than written: a late verdict describes a root that is no longer there, and
    committing it would flicker the form back to what was typed before. A pass
    that throws leaves the previous verdict standing — an error reaching the
    network is not evidence that the form became acceptable.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">The packages</h2>
  <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="text-left text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="hairline">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Package</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-medium">What it is</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          packages.map(([name, what]) => (
            <tr class="hairline">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 align-top font-mono text-xs whitespace-nowrap">
                {name}
              </td>
              <td class="py-3 align-top leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
                {what}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-outline-variant p-5 leading-relaxed">
    <strong>Nothing here is published yet.</strong> The packages are npm
    workspaces, so they resolve to each other with no linking step; build from
    source with <code>npm install</code> and <code>npm run verify</code>.
  </p>
</Base>
